onship between sarcopenia and prognosis in liver cirrhosis patients.NARDELLI S:Nardelli S is an author who studied the impact of sarcopenia on hepatic encephalopathy development.TORRISI S:Torrisi S is an author who studied the relationship between sarcopenia and hepatic encephalopathy.ENGLESBE MJ:Englesbe MJ is an author who researched the impact of sarcopenia on mortality after liver transplantation.PATEL SP:Patel SP is an author who studied the relationship between sarcopenia and liver transplantation outcomes.MEZA-JUNCO J:Meza-Junco J is an author who contributed to research on sarcopenia and liver cirrhosis.PRADO CM:Prado CM is an author who studied the impact of nutritional status on liver transplantation outcomes.IRITANI S:Iritani S is an author who researched skeletal muscle depletion as a prognostic factor for hepatocellular carcinoma.IMAI K:Imai K is an author involved in research on skeletal muscle depletion and liver cancer.TAKAI K:Takai K is an author who studied the impact of skeletal muscle depletion on liver cancer prognosis.NEY M:Ney M is an author who conducted a meta-analysis on nutritional supplementation in cirrhosis.VANDERMEER B:Vandermeer B is an author who co-authored a meta-analysis on nutritional supplementation in liver disease.VAN ZANTEN SJ:Van Zanten SJ is an author involved in research on nutritional supplementation in cirrhosis.GRAMLICH L:Gramlich L is an author recognized for contributions to the field of nutrition, particularly in surgical contexts. They played a significant role in the consensus recommendations from the North American Surgical Nutrition Summit, highlighting their expertise in nutritional practices related to surgery. Additionally, Gramlich L has conducted research on the effects of nutritional supplementation in liver disease, further demonstrating their commitment to advancing knowledge in nutritional health and its implications for various medical conditions.CABR√â E:Cabr√© E is an author involved in the ESPEN guidelines on nutritional support for liver disease.MARCHESINI G:Marchesini G is an author who contributed to the ESPEN guidelines on nutritional support in hepatology.SCH√úTZ T:Sch√ºtz T is an author involved in the ESPEN guidelines on nutritional support for liver disease.SHENKIN A:Shenkin A is an author who contributed to the ESPEN guidelines on parenteral nutrition in hepatology.WENDON J:Wendon J is an author involved in the ESPEN guidelines on nutritional support for liver disease.M√òLLER S:DIETARY SODIUM RESTRICTION:Dietary sodium restriction is a dietary approach often recommended for patients with liver cirrhosis to manage fluid retention and other complications.BRANCHED CHAIN AMINO ACIDS:Branched chain amino acids (BCAAs) are essential nutrients that the body obtains from proteins found in food, often used in supplementation for patients with liver disease.MJA:The Medical Journal of Australia (MJA) is a peer-reviewed medical journal that publishes research and reviews on various health topics, including liver diseases.NUTR CLIN PRACT:"NUTR CLIN PRACT" is a journal dedicated to the field of clinical nutrition and its significant implications for patient care. It encompasses a wide range of topics, including the management of chyle leaks and the nutritional needs of burn patients. The journal serves as a platform for guidelines and research related to clinical nutrition practices, emphasizing the role of nutrition in various clinical settings, including studies pertinent to liver health. Notably, it is also the publication venue for Krenitsky's work on adjusted body weight, further highlighting its commitment to advancing knowledge in clinical nutrition. Overall, "NUTR CLIN PRACT" plays a crucial role in disseminating research and best practices in the field of clinical nutrition.SHIRAKI M:Shiraki M is a researcher involved in the study of fuel metabolism in patients with liver cirrhosis.MURAKAMI N:Murakami N is a researcher who studied the effects of dietary interventions in liver cirrhosis.SWART GR:Swart GR is a researcher who studied the effects of late evening meals on patients with liver cirrhosis.AOYAMA K:Aoyama K is a researcher who studied the effects of late evening snacks on outpatients with liver cirrhosis.TSIEN CD:Tsien CD is a researcher who explored the metabolic responses to late evening snacks in cirrhosis patients.GU XB:Gu XB is a researcher who studied the effects of dietary sodium on ascites in liver cirrhosis patients.ZHU YF:Zhu YF is a researcher who investigated the influence of sodium-restricted diets on patients with cirrhotic ascites.SOULSBY CT:Soulsby CT is a researcher who studied the effects of dietary sodium restriction on energy and protein intake in cirrhosis patients.SEYMOUR CA:Seymour CA is a researcher who discussed dietary management of hepatic encephalopathy.LIVER DISEASE:Liver disease encompasses a variety of conditions that impact the liver's function and structure, including serious ailments such as cirrhosis and liver cancer. It can also manifest in patients with cystic fibrosis, highlighting the need for specialized care and management tailored to this specific patient population.SAN FRANCISCO:San Francisco is a city in California where the DDW event took place in 2002, focusing on liver disease research.AMERICAN COLLEGE OF NUTRITION:The American College of Nutrition is a professional organization that focuses on the study and application of nutrition science.BRANCHED-CHAIN AMINO ACIDS:Branched-chain amino acids are essential nutrients that the body obtains from proteins found in food, often used in nutritional supplementation for liver disease.HOSPITAL VIRGEN DE LA VICTORIA:Hospital Virgen de la Victoria is a clinical hospital specializing in endocrinology and clinical nutrition located in M√°laga, Spain.HOSPITAL QUIR√ìN-SALUD:Hospital Quir√≥n-salud is a clinical hospital specializing in endocrinology and clinical nutrition located in M√°laga, Spain.NUTRITIONAL STATE:Nutritional state refers to the condition of the body in relation to the intake and utilization of nutrients, which can be affected by various health conditions, including liver diseases.POST-LIVER TRANSPLANTATION SARCOPENIA:Post-liver transplantation sarcopenia is a condition characterized by loss of muscle mass and strength following liver transplantation, often evaluated in patients with liver cirrhosis.ARETAEUS DE CAPADOCIA:Aretaeus de Capadocia was a physician in the second half of the 2nd century BC who first described the condition known as celiac disease.SAMUEL GEE:Samuel Gee was a physician who provided a more contemporary clinical report on celiac disease in 1888, emphasizing the importance of dietary regulation in treatment.GLUTEN DICKE:Gluten Dicke was a researcher who made a significant discovery regarding the relationship between gluten consumption and celiac disease after World War II.HLA DQ2:HLA DQ2 is a genetic marker associated with a higher predisposition to celiac disease and related conditions.HLA DQ8:HLA DQ8 is another genetic marker linked to an increased risk of developing celiac disease.MINERALS:Minerals are inorganic elements that are essential for human health and play a crucial role in various bodily functions. They are commonly found in dietary supplements and are vital for maintaining overall well-being. Additionally, the absorption of these inorganic nutrients can be impaired in individuals with celiac disease, highlighting the importance of understanding mineral intake and its impact on health.HYPERPLASIA:Hyperplasia is an increase in the number of cells, which can occur in the intestinal mucosa of individuals with celiac disease.ATROPHY:Atrophy refers to the wasting away or decrease in size of the intestinal villi, a characteristic of celiac disease.IMMUNE RESPONSE:The immune response is the body's reaction to gluten in individuals with celiac disease, leading to inflammation and damage.LYMPHOCYTES:Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell involved in the immune response, which plays a role in the pathology of celiac disease.ANTIGLIADIN ANTIBODIES:Antigliadin antibodies are markers that can indicate an autoimmune response to gluten in individuals with celiac disease.ANTIENDOMYSIAL ANTIBODIES:Antiendomysial antibodies are another type of antibody that can be present in individuals with celiac disease, indicating intestinal damage.ANTIRETICULIN ANTIBODIES:Antireticulin antibodies are associated with celiac disease and can be used as a diagnostic marker for the condition.GLUTEN SENSITIVITY:Gluten sensitivity is a condition characterized by individuals experiencing symptoms related to gluten consumption. It is distinct from celiac disease, meaning that those with gluten sensitivity do not have the autoimmune response associated with celiac disease.ICEBERG CELIAC:The iceberg celiac theory suggests that many individuals with celiac disease may remain undiagnosed, with only a small percentage showing classic symptoms.)<|COMPLETE|>CEREAL:Cereal is a type of grain that is rich in carbohydrates and includes various species consumed by humans such as wheat, barley, and corn.HARINA DE CEREAL:Flour made from cereal grains, primarily used in baking and cooking.SENSIBILIDAD AL GLUTEN NO CELIACA:SENSIBILIDAD AL GLUTEN NO CELIACA, or non-celiac gluten sensitivity, is a condition characterized by gastrointestinal symptoms that occur in response to gluten consumption. Unlike celiac disease, individuals with this sensitivity do not exhibit the same diagnostic markers associated with celiac disease. This distinction highlights that while both conditions involve adverse reactions to gluten, they are fundamentally different in their underlying mechanisms and diagnostic criteria.CORN:Corn is a cereal grain that is widely used in various food products and is gluten-free.BARLEY:Barley is a cereal grain that contains gluten and can trigger symptoms in individuals with celiac disease.VITAMINAS DEL GRUPO B:B vitamins are a group of water-soluble vitamins that play important roles in cell metabolism and are found in various cereals.S√çNTOMAS EXTRAINTENSTINALES:Extraintestinal symptoms are non-digestive reactions that can occur in individuals with celiac disease, such as fatigue and headaches.DIETA LIBRE DE GLUTEN:The "DIETA LIBRE DE GLUTEN" refers to a gluten-free diet, which is a dietary regimen that excludes foods containing gluten. This diet is primarily adopted by individuals diagnosed with celiac disease or those who experience gluten sensitivity. Research has shown that a gluten-free diet can also be beneficial for some patients suffering from irritable bowel syndrome and gluten sensitivity, as indicated by clinical trials. It is particularly recommended for patients with celiac disease to help manage and control the symptoms associated with this condition.HUMANO:HUMANO refers to humans, who are classified as primates. One notable characteristic of humans is the absence of the enzyme uricase, which results in elevated levels of uric acid in their bodies compared to other mammals. Additionally, humans are susceptible to celiac disease and gluten sensitivity, conditions that can trigger a range of symptoms when gluten is consumed.DIABETES MELLITUS 1:La diabetes mellitus tipo 1 es una enfermedad autoinmune que se ha asociado con la enfermedad cel√≠aca, presentando un riesgo mayor en pacientes cel√≠acos.ENFERMEDAD DE SJ√ñGREN:La enfermedad de Sj√∂gren es otra patolog√≠a autoinmune que puede estar asociada con la enfermedad cel√≠aca.INFERTILIDAD:La infertilidad puede ser una complicaci√≥n en pacientes con enfermedad cel√≠aca, a menudo relacionada con deficiencias nutricionales.ABORTOS:Los abortos de repetici√≥n pueden estar asociados con la enfermedad cel√≠aca, debido a problemas de malnutrici√≥n y autoinmunidad.TRASTORNOS NEUROPSIQUI√ÅTRICOS:Los trastornos neuropsiqui√°tricos son manifestaciones que pueden asociarse con la enfermedad cel√≠aca, incluyendo ataxia, cefaleas y trastornos ansioso-depresivos.ARTRITIS:Artritis es una condici√≥n que puede aparecer en un porcentaje significativo de adultos que padecen enfermedad cel√≠aca, lo que sugiere una relaci√≥n entre ambas condiciones.DERMATITIS HERPETIFORME:Dermatitis herpetiforme es una erupci√≥n cut√°nea que se asocia con la enfermedad cel√≠aca. Esta condici√≥n se manifiesta a trav√©s de lesiones ves√≠culo-papulosas y pruriginosas, afectando de manera sim√©trica las extremidades y la regi√≥n gl√∫tea.SINDROME DE DOWN:El s√≠ndrome de Down es una condici√≥n gen√©tica que se ha asociado con un riesgo significativamente mayor de desarrollar enfermedad cel√≠aca.ENFERMEDAD TIROIDEA:Los trastornos tiroideos autoinmunes, como el hipotiroidismo, son m√°s frecuentes en pacientes con enfermedad cel√≠aca.ENFERMEDAD HEPATICA:La enfermedad hep√°tica puede presentarse en pacientes con enfermedad cel√≠aca, incluyendo hepatitis autoinmune y cirrosis biliar primaria.MIOCARDITIS Y MIOCARDIOPATIA DILATADA:La miocarditis y la miocardiopat√≠a dilatada son condiciones card√≠acas que pueden asociarse raramente con la enfermedad cel√≠aca.HIPOESPLENISMO:El hipoesplenismo es una condici√≥n que se ha descrito en casos asociados a la enfermedad cel√≠aca, aumentando el riesgo de infecciones severas.D√âFICIT SELECTIVO DE IGA:El d√©ficit selectivo de IgA aparece en un 1-2% de los pacientes cel√≠acos, lo que indica una posible relaci√≥n con la enfermedad cel√≠aca.RIESGO DE NEOPLASIAS:El riesgo de aparici√≥n de ciertos tumores, como linfoma intestinal no-Hodgkin, parece estar aumentado en pacientes con enfermedad cel√≠aca.ALTERACIONES EN LAS FANERAS:Las alteraciones en las faneras, como piel, pelo y u√±as, pueden ser s√≠ntomas de deficiencias nutricionales en pacientes cel√≠acos.S√çNDROME DE DOWN:Trastorno gen√©tico que puede estar asociado a un mayor riesgo de enfermedad cel√≠aca.S√çNDROME DE WILLIAMS:Trastorno gen√©tico que puede estar asociado a un mayor riesgo de enfermedad cel√≠aca.ANTICUERPOS IGA ANTIENDOMISIO:Prueba serol√≥gica utilizada para el diagn√≥stico de la enfermedad cel√≠aca, con alta sensibilidad y especificidad.ANTICUERPOS IGA ANTITRANSGLUTAMINASA:Prueba serol√≥gica utilizada para el diagn√≥stico de la enfermedad cel√≠aca, con alta sensibilidad y especificidad.ANTICUERPOS IGA ANTIGLIADINA:Prueba serol√≥gica utilizada para el diagn√≥stico de la enfermedad cel√≠aca, con sensibilidad y especificidad moderadas.ANTICUERPOS IGG ANTIGLIADINA:Prueba serol√≥gica utilizada para el diagn√≥stico de la enfermedad cel√≠aca, con sensibilidad y especificidad moderadas.PACIENTES CELIACOS:ANTICUERPOS EMA:Los anticuerpos EMA son marcadores serol√≥gicos utilizados en el diagn√≥stico de la enfermedad cel√≠aca, indicando la presencia de una respuesta inmune al gluten.ANTICUERPOS TTG:Los anticuerpos antitransglutaminasa (tTG) son pruebas serol√≥gicas que ayudan a diagnosticar la enfermedad cel√≠aca al detectar la respuesta inmune al gluten.D-XILOSA:La D-xilosa es un monosac√°rido utilizado en pruebas para evaluar la absorci√≥n intestinal y la integridad de la mucosa intestinal en el diagn√≥stico de malabsorci√≥n.DIETISTA:A dietitian is a healthcare professional who specializes in nutrition and diet planning, particularly for patients with specific dietary needs. This expertise is especially crucial for individuals with conditions such as celiac disease, as a specialized dietitian can tailor dietary recommendations to meet their unique requirements.CEBADA:La cebada es otro cereal que contiene gluten y es perjudicial para los pacientes con celiaqu√≠a.AMARANTO:El amaranto es un cereal sin gluten que se puede consumir de forma segura por los pacientes con celiaqu√≠a.MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM:Un equipo multidisciplinario que proporciona seguimiento continuo a pacientes con enfermedad celiaca para supervisar la efectividad del tratamiento.CALORIC REQUIREMENTS:Requerimientos cal√≥ricos que deben ser calculados y adaptados a las necesidades individuales de los pacientes con enfermedad celiaca.DIETARY HABITS:Dietary habits refer to the patterns of food consumption that can significantly impact health outcomes, including cardiovascular health. These habits encompass various aspects of an individual's diet, including the types of foods consumed and the frequency of consumption. Additionally, it is important to consider the previous dietary habits of patients, particularly when adapting to specific dietary requirements, such as following a gluten-free diet. Respecting and adapting these prior habits is crucial for ensuring compliance and promoting overall health in individuals making dietary changes.MINISTERIO DE SANIDAD DE ESPA√ëA:The Ministry of Health of Spain oversees public health policies and provides guidelines regarding celiac disease and gluten-free diets.CELIAC SPRUE ASSOCIATION:The Celiac Sprue Association is a U.S.-based organization that provides support and resources for individuals with celiac disease.NATIONAL INSTITUTE OF DIABETES AND DIGESTIVE AND KIDNEY DISEASES:The National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK) is a component of the National Institutes of Health (NIH) that focuses on conducting research and disseminating information related to various health issues. This institute is dedicated to studying digestive diseases, including celiac disease, and also provides valuable information on lactose intolerance. Through its research initiatives and educational resources, the NIDDK aims to enhance understanding and treatment of conditions affecting diabetes, digestive health, and kidney function.GLUTEN INTOLERANCE GROUP:The Gluten Intolerance Group is an organization that offers support and resources for individuals with gluten-related disorders.SCOTT ADAMS ASSOCIATION:An association that supports individuals with celiac disease and promotes awareness about gluten-free diets.EUROPEAN COELIAC SOCIETIES:An association that represents various organizations across Europe focused on celiac disease and gluten-free living.GLUTEN-FREE DIET:The gluten-free diet is a dietary regimen that excludes gluten, a protein found in wheat, barley, and rye. This diet is essential for managing celiac disease and gluten intolerance, as it helps individuals avoid the adverse health effects associated with the consumption of gluten.HARINA DE MA√çZ:Corn flour is a gluten-free alternative to wheat flour that can be used in various recipes.HARINA DE PATATAS:Potato flour is another gluten-free substitute that can be used in cooking and baking.HARINA DE ARROZ:Rice flour is a gluten-free flour option that is often used in gluten-free recipes.ALMID√ìN DE PATATA:Potato starch is a gluten-free thickening agent that can be used in cooking.HARINA DE SOJA:Soy flour is a gluten-free alternative that is high in protein and can be used in various recipes.HARINA DE TRIGO:DIETA CONTROLADA EN GLUTEN:The "DIETA CONTROLADA EN GLUTEN" is a dietary regimen that excludes foods containing gluten. This diet is primarily recommended for individuals with celiac disease, a condition that necessitates strict avoidance of gluten to prevent adverse health effects. It is utilized mainly by those diagnosed with this condition to manage their symptoms and maintain overall health.ALIMENTOS SIN GLUTEN:"ALIMENTOS SIN GLUTEN" refers to foods that do not contain gluten, making them safe for consumption by individuals with celiac disease. These gluten-free foods are specifically designed to meet the dietary needs of those who cannot tolerate gluten, ensuring their health and well-being.HARINAS DE TRIGO:Las harinas de trigo son un ingrediente b√°sico en muchos productos de panader√≠a y contienen gluten, lo que las hace inapropiadas para personas con enfermedad celiaca.BOLLOS:Los bollos son productos de pasteler√≠a que generalmente contienen gluten y deben ser evitados por personas con enfermedad celiaca.PASTAS ITALIANAS:Las pastas italianas, como fideos y macarrones, son alimentos que contienen gluten y deben ser evitados por personas con enfermedad celiaca.LECHE Y DERIVADOS:La leche y sus derivados son alimentos que no contienen gluten y son seguros para el consumo de personas con enfermedad celiaca.QUESOS FUNDIDOS:Los quesos fundidos de sabores pueden contener gluten y deben ser verificados antes de su consumo por personas con enfermedad celiaca.PAT√âS:Los pat√©s diversos pueden contener gluten y deben ser consumidos con precauci√≥n por personas con enfermedad celiaca.CONSERVAS DE CARNES:Las conservas de carnes pueden contener gluten y deben ser verificadas por personas con enfermedad celiaca.CONSERVAS DE PESCADOS:Las conservas de pescados con distintas salsas pueden contener gluten y deben ser consumidas con precauci√≥n por personas con enfermedad celiaca.SUCED√ÅNEOS DE CAF√â:Los suced√°neos de caf√© y otras bebidas de m√°quina pueden contener gluten y deben ser verificados antes de su consumo por personas con enfermedad celiaca.FRUTOS SECOS TOSTADOS:Los frutos secos tostados con sal pueden contener gluten y deben ser consumidos con precauci√≥n por personas con enfermedad celiaca.SUCED√ÅNEOS DE CHOCOLATE:Los suced√°neos de chocolate pueden contener gluten y deben ser verificados antes de su consumo por personas con enfermedad celiaca.COLORANTE ALIMENTARIO:El colorante alimentario puede contener gluten y debe ser verificado antes de su uso por personas con enfermedad celiaca.GLUTEN-FREE CERTIFICATION ORGANIZATION:The Gluten-Free Certification Organization is an entity established to certify food products as gluten-free, adhering to stringent criteria. Its primary mission is to provide certification for gluten-free products, ensuring safety and reliability for individuals with celiac disease and other gluten-related disorders.CODIGO ALIMENTARIO:The Codex Alimentarius is a collection of internationally recognized standards, guidelines, and codes of practice to ensure food safety and quality.PROTE√çNAS Y AMINO√ÅCIDOS:Prote√≠nas y Amino√°cidos is a section discussing the content of gluten in various food groups, particularly focusing on proteins and amino acids.ALIMENTOS RECOMENDADOS:Alimentos recomendados refers to food items that do not contain gluten and are safe for individuals with celiac disease.FARIN√ÅCEOS:Farin√°ceos refers to a group of food items, including bread and cereals, that are typically high in gluten and should be avoided.TUB√âRCULOS Y DERIVADOS:Tub√©rculos y derivados refers to food items that can replace gluten-containing cereals in a diet.)<|COMPLETE|>BONIATOS:Los boniatos son un tipo de tub√©rculo recomendado en la dieta controlada en gluten, preferidos en su forma fresca.PATATAS RELLENAS:Las patatas rellenas de ketchup y/o mostaza son un alimento a evitar en la dieta controlada en gluten.PUR√â DE PATATA INSTANT√ÅNEO:El pur√© de patata instant√°neo es un alimento que puede ser consumido en la dieta controlada en gluten si se elige la versi√≥n adecuada.CARNES EN CONSERVA:Las carnes en conserva son un tipo de alimento a evitar en la dieta controlada en gluten, ya que pueden contener gluten.PESCADOS REBOZADOS:Los pescados rebozados son un tipo de alimento a evitar en la dieta controlada en gluten, ya que contienen gluten.SUCED√ÅNEOS DE MARISCO:Los suced√°neos de marisco son un tipo de alimento a evitar en la dieta controlada en gluten, ya que pueden contener gluten.CONSERVAS DE PESCADO:Las conservas de pescado en salsa o con tomate son un tipo de alimento a evitar en la dieta controlada en gluten.(N␀␀␀␀␀␀ñN␀␀␀␀␀␀ˆN␀␀␀␀␀␀lO␀␀␀␀␀␀ÃO␀␀␀␀␀␀/P␀␀␀␀␀␀¶P␀␀␀␀␀␀
